------------------------------------
IIB (Instance Initialization Block)
----------------------------------
IIB executes only when an object is created.
No object created → IIB is not executed.
If 2 objects are created → IIB executes 2 times.
IIB executes before the constructor.
If there are multiple IIBs, they execute in the order they are written.
An IIB can initialize both static and non-static variables
	(though static variables are typically initialized in a static block).

class IIBDemo {
    static int companyId;  ------> Static variable
    int employeeId;	   ------> Instance variable

    static { -----> Static Initialization Block (executes only once)
        companyId = 100;
        System.out.println("Static Block Executed");
    }

    { -----> Instance Initialization Block 1
        System.out.println("IIB 1 Executed");
        employeeId = 1;      // Initializing instance variable
        companyId = 200;     // IIB can also modify static variable
    }

    { -----> Instance Initialization Block 2
        System.out.println("IIB 2 Executed");
    }

    IIBDemo() { ----> Constructor
        System.out.println("Constructor Executed");
        System.out.println("Company ID : " + companyId);
        System.out.println("Employee ID: " + employeeId);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Creating First Object");
        IIBDemo obj1 = new IIBDemo();

        System.out.println();

        System.out.println("Creating Second Object");
        IIBDemo obj2 = new IIBDemo();
    }
}

Output-
------
Static Block Executed
Creating First Object
IIB 1 Executed
IIB 2 Executed
Constructor Executed
Company ID : 200
Employee ID: 1

Creating Second Object
IIB 1 Executed
IIB 2 Executed
Constructor Executed
Company ID : 200
Employee ID: 1


---------------------------------
SIB (Static Initialization Block)
---------------------------------
SIB is declared inside a class using the static keyword.
Executes once when the class is loaded, before the main() method.
No object creation is required for SIB to execute.
If there are multiple SIBs, they execute in the order they are written.
SIB is mainly used to initialize static variables.
SIB cannot directly access or initialize non-static (instance) variables because no object exists yet.
SIB executes before IIB, constructors, and object creation.


class SIBDemo {
    static int companyId;   ------> Static variable
    int employeeId = 10;    ------>Instance variable

    static {----> Static Initialization Block 1
        System.out.println("SIB 1 Executed");
        companyId = 100;   // Initializing static variable
    }

    static {----> Static Initialization Block 2
        System.out.println("SIB 2 Executed");
        companyId = 200;
    }
 
    { -----> Instance Initialization Block
        System.out.println("IIB Executed");
    }

    SIBDemo() { ------> Constructor
        System.out.println("Constructor Executed");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Main Method Started");
        System.out.println("Company ID = " + companyId);

        System.out.println("\nCreating First Object...");
        new SIBDemo();

        System.out.println("\nCreating Second Object...");
        new SIBDemo();
    }
}

