----------------------
i. Overriding/Runtime:
---------------------
Method overriding is a feature in Java where a child class provides its own implementation
of a method that is already defined in the parent class with the same method signature
(same name, parameters, and return type compatibility).
The method to execute is decided at runtime based on the actual object type.

note: Constructor cannot be overridden ❌ because constructors are not inherited.

class A{
     A(){};
     void display(){};
     void showData(){};
}

class B extends A{
    /*B(){--------> Constructor cannot be overridden ❌
        super()--------> calls class A constructor using super()
	System.out.println("class B constructor implementation");
    }; */

    @Override
    void display(){ -------> class A display method implementation
       System.out.println("Class A method implementation");
    }

    /* @Override
    String showData(){ -------> // ❌ Compile-time error, Cannot override showData() because the return type is incompatible.
       return "Hello";
    }*/
}

Covariant Return Type (Allowed)
----------------------------------
class Animal {}

class Dog extends Animal {}

class Parent {
    Animal getAnimal() {
        return new Animal();
    }
}

class Child extends Parent {
    @Override
    Dog getAnimal() {
