ii. Overloading/Compile-Time
-----------------------------
Method overloading is a feature in Java where a class contains multiple methods with
the same name but different parameter lists (number, type, or order of parameters).
The compiler decides which method to call at compile time, so it is called compile-time
polymorphism.

class A{
   A(){};  -------------------------------> default / no-argument constructor
   A(int x, int y){}; --------------------> parameterized constructor
   void print(){}; -----------------------> method with no parameters
   void print(String name){}; ------------> Method with one parameter
   public print(String name, int age){};----> Method with two parameters & with modifier public
}

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
|		Type	         |              Example			       |
|--------------------------------|---------------------------------------------|
| Different number of parameters | add(int,int) and add(int,int,int)	       |
| Different parameter data types | add(int,int) and add(double,double)	       |
| Different order of parameters  | details(String,int) and details(int,String) |
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Method overloading depends on:
✅ Number of parameters
✅ Type of parameters
✅ Order of parameters

It does not depend on:
❌ Return type
❌ Access modifier (public, private, etc.)


---------------------------------------------------------
| Compile-Time Polymorphism | Runtime Polymorphism      |
| ------------------------- | ------------------------- |
| Method Overloading        | Method Overriding         |
| Decided by compiler       | Decided by JVM at runtime |
| Same class                | Parent-child relationship |
| Static binding            | Dynamic binding           |
| Faster                    | Slightly slower           |
---------------------------------------------------------

