String Pool or String Intern Pool
---------------------------------------------------
special memory area -> that store "String literals"
helps to optimize memory usage and performance when dealing with strings.
or
The String Pool is a special area inside the JVM Heap where Java stores only
one copy of each unique string literal.
When the same literal is used again, Java reuses the existing object instead of
creating a new one.

-> intern() method :
used to add string to the pool explicitly
if the string already in the pool intern() returns the existing reference.


String s1 = "Java";  -------> Stored in String Pool
String s2 = "Java";  -------> Reuses the same object from String Pool

s1 == s2   -----------------> true -> same reference (same address)
s1.equals(s2) --------------> true -> same content (value)


String s3 = new String("Java"); ------> New object in Heap memory
String s4 = new String("Java"); ------> Another new object in Heap memory

s3 == s4 -------------------> false-> different references (different addresses)
s3.equals(s4)---------------> true -> same content (value)


String s5 = new String("Java").intern();---------> Returns String Pool reference

s1 == s5 ---------------> true  -> same reference (same address)
s1.equals(s5)-----------> true  -> same content (value)


