-> Key Difference
--------------
---------------------------------------------------------
| Aspect        |   HashMap reference |   Map reference |
| ------------- |-------------------- | --------------- |
| Type          | Concrete class      | Interface       |
| Flexibility   | Low                 | High            |
| Coupling      | Tight coupling      | Loose coupling  |
| Best practice | Not recommended     | Recommended     |
---------------------------------------------------------

Why Map is Preferred?
------------------------
Map<Integer, String> map = new HashMap<>(); is preferred because it follows
programming to an interface, which provides flexibility to change implementations
like LinkedHashMap or TreeMap without modifying code.
Using HashMap<Integer, String> directly creates tight coupling to a
specific implementation.

Does HashMap convert the entire structure into a Red-Black Tree
   when collisions occur, or is the conversion done only for a single bucket?
-----------------------------------------------
HashMap does not convert the entire structure into a Red-Black Tree.
Only a single bucket is treeified when the number of nodes in that
bucket exceeds 8 and the capacity is at least 64. Other buckets
remain unchanged.

